    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Various types of geological structures are commonly present in the same rock volume in the fields, for example, in the Aztec Sandstone cropping out in the Valley of Fire State Park, NV. In this paper, we numerically studied the fluid flow through a porous sandstone with subparallel and intersecting joint and compaction band (CB) sets. Numerical analyses show that if one joint set is subparallel to one CB set, the combined system has little effect on the fluid flow in the direction normal to the CB set with respect to a case of the one isolated set of CB. However, if the joint set is normal to the CB set, the combined system can significantly enhance the fluid flow in the direction normal to the CB set with respect to the isolated CB set. The results are between these two limiting situations when the intersection angles of joint and CB sets are between 0o and 90o. These results confirm the importance of representing various types of geological structures with their proper geometries and distributions in subsurface fluid flow models.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
